Q: Optimizing CPU Usage in Java Regex Matching A: <p><a href="https://github.com/alexey-pelykh/pcre4j" rel="nofollow noreferrer">pcre4j</a> can help on two fronts here:</p>
<p><strong>1. JIT compilation.</strong> pcre4j uses the PCRE2 engine, which JIT-compiles regex patterns to native machine code.
For CPU-bound regex workloads, this can significantly reduce matching time compared to <code>java.util.regex</code>'s
interpreted bytecode approach.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>import org.pcre4j.regex.Pattern;
import org.pcre4j.regex.Matcher;

// JIT compilation happens automatically
Pattern p = Pattern.compile(&quot;your-pattern-here&quot;);
Matcher m = p.matcher(input);
</code></pre>
<ol start="2">
<li>Match limits. If the real problem is catastrophic backtracking (a pattern that takes exponential time on
certain inputs), pcre4j exposes PCRE2's safety limits:</li>
</ol>
<ul>
<li>Match limit — caps internal match function calls</li>
<li>Depth limit — caps recursion depth</li>
<li>Heap limit — caps memory during matching</li>
</ul>
<p>When a limit is hit, the match fails fast with an error instead of spinning the CPU.</p>
<p>These can be set globally via system properties (pcre2.regex.match.limit) or per-pattern.</p>
<p>The regex module is a drop-in replacement for java.util.regex — same Pattern/Matcher API.</p>
<p>Maven:</p>
<pre class="lang-xml prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;dependency&gt;
    &lt;groupId&gt;org.pcre4j&lt;/groupId&gt;
    &lt;artifactId&gt;regex&lt;/artifactId&gt;
    &lt;version&gt;1.0.0&lt;/version&gt;
&lt;/dependency&gt;
&lt;dependency&gt;
    &lt;groupId&gt;org.pcre4j&lt;/groupId&gt;
    &lt;artifactId&gt;jna&lt;/artifactId&gt;
    &lt;version&gt;1.0.0&lt;/version&gt;
&lt;/dependency&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Requires Java 21+.</p>
<p><em>Disclosure: I am the author of pcre4j.</em></p>
